<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1082">1082</a>–1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Ramon Berenguer the Great · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramon Berenguer III, called the Great, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1086 to 1131. He gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besalu">Besalú</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> by inheritance and, through his marriage to Douce in 1112, the County of Provence. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> he led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1114 to 1115, sacking the island before withdrawing. He became a Templar shortly before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona.jpg" alt="Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona" width="207" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramon_Berenguer_IV_(Barcelona).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramon Berenguer III was born on 11 November 1082 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodez">Rodez</a>, the son of Ramon Berenguer II. He succeeded his father as count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> and Ausona in 1086, ruling jointly with his uncle Berenguer Ramon II, and became sole ruler in 1097 when his uncle was forced into exile. His first wife was María Rodríguez de Vivar, second daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>, who died around 1105 and left three daughters. His second wife, Almodis, had no children. His third wife was Douce, heiress of Provence, with whom he had seven children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1102 he answered increased Almoravid raids on his lands with a counter-attack, helped by Ermengol V, Count of Urgell, but was defeated at Mollerussa, where Ermengol was killed. By marriage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> and inheritance he brought almost all the Catalan counties, except Urgell and Peralada, into his realm. He inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besalu">Besalú</a> in 1111 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> in 1117, and through his marriage to Douce in 1112 took Provence, so that his lands stretched as far east as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. With the Count of Urgell he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a>.</p><p>He established relations with the maritime republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. In 1114 and 1115 he attacked the then-Muslim islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> and Ibiza with Pisa, and they became his tributaries, with many Christian slaves recovered and freed. With Pisa's help he also raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a>. In 1116 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to ask Pope Paschal II for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, which by 1118 he had captured and rebuilt as the metropolitan seat of the church in Catalonia, which had before depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>. In 1127 he signed a commercial treaty with Genoa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 14 July 1130 he became an associate member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>. He gave his five Catalan counties to his eldest son Ramon Berenguer IV, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronilla of Aragon</a>, and Provence to his younger son Berenguer Ramon. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1131, the sources giving 23 January or 19 July, and was buried at the monastery of Santa Maria de Ripoll.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ramon Berenguer III</b> gained Provence by marriage, and the city prospered through trade and tribute from the taifas.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-barcelona">County of Barcelona</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Bernat III married a daughter of <b>Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</b> and died without heirs in 1111, so the county passed to Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-besalu">County of Besalú</a></li><li><blockquote>When Count Bernat Guillem died without heirs in 1117, Cerdanya passed to <b>Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-cerdanya">County of Cerdanya</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1112 the heiress Douce married <b>Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</b>, bringing Catalan counts who encouraged troubadour culture and Mediterranean trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-provence">County of Provence</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1113-1115 a fleet of Pisans and Catalans under <b>Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</b> sacked Ibiza and Madina Mayurqa, but withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-majorca">Taifa of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodez">Rodez</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.35,2.57&amp;year=1113" aria-label="Rodez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5157 3784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5157 3784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.35,2.57&amp;year=1113">What was here in 1113? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
